# Data and integrations

## Environment variables

- Every configurable URL, key, and path lives in `.env` locally and in the
  deploy environment: `API_URL`, `AUTH_API_URL`, `API_KEY`,
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_GRADER_URL`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_GRADER_API_URL`,
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_GRADER_SEARCH_PATHS`.
- The public site origin is baked into `design-system/site.ts` (`SITE_URL`),
  same pattern as customer sites — not a deploy secret.
- Required variables have no in-code defaults. A module that needs one reads
  it through a `required(name, value)` guard that throws at build time with the
  variable's name (`design-system/lib/grader.ts` is the model). A missing
  variable fails the build, never a request.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are inlined at build time: spell each read out as a
  literal `process.env.NAME`; never look one up dynamically.
- Adding a variable means adding it to `.env`, to the guard, and to this list.

## The `@keystone-sites/*` packages

- `@keystone-sites/core` — server data (`lib/server-api`: company information,
  team, portfolio), entity types, the chat and form route handlers, the chat
  backend resolver. Server-only; never import it in a client component.
- `@keystone-sites/widgets` — the battle-tested Keystone widgets (`ChatWidget`
  and friends).
- `@keystone-sites/services` — a transitive widgets runtime dependency. It is
  not imported by site code, but remains in `transpilePackages` because the
  widget package consumes its TypeScript source.

Data from `server-api` is typed at the page or section boundary against the
package's entity types before it reaches a component. Helpers that return
`unknown` or loosely typed objects are validated at the fetch site; only
verified fields render.

## Sitemap and social destinations

`design-system/site-links.ts` is the source of truth for site-owned routes and
external product destinations. An assigned sitemap route is not dead code
merely because its page is authored separately; preserve it unless the sitemap
itself changes. Company social links come only from backend company
information. Omit an unavailable network; never add a static social fallback
or `href="#"`.

## The chat widget

The site chat is `@keystone-sites/widgets` `ChatWidget`, mounted once in the
root layout by `sections/site-chat.tsx` and configured by
`sections/site-chat-data.ts` (accent, placeholder, suggested questions).
Business name, logo, and team come from `server-api`; it renders only when the
account has chat enabled.

- **Configure it through its accepted props only.** Never alter its layout,
  controls, spacing, typography, or markup. The documented prompt-only color
  bridge in `widgets.css` may map `--ks-chat-accent-ink` and prompt SVG ink to
  site tokens; no other class override is allowed. Its interaction surface is
  standardized across every Keystone site by design.
- The widget ships Tailwind v4 classes with no compiled CSS. The host compiles
  them in `design-system/widgets.css` and nowhere else: theme + utilities,
  no preflight (`base.css` is the equivalent contract), `source(none)` so the
  site tree is never scanned, and the utilities emitted **unlayered** so a
  class beats the site's unlayered element reset. Utilities in `@layer
  utilities` lose to `* { padding: 0 }` and `button { background: transparent }`
  and the widget renders broken. Do not move that import into a layer.
- Site markup never uses Tailwind class names. The compile only matches the
  widget's own markup.
- Opening the chat from the site is a `data-action="open-chat"` contract:
  `sections/site-chat-open.tsx` delegates the click to the widget's own prompt
  pill, so the widget runs its standard behaviour (suggested questions for a
  new session, the conversation for a returning one). Never reimplement widget
  state.
- Shared variable names between the Tailwind theme and the site tokens
  (`--radius-*`, `--font-serif`, `--font-weight-*`) resolve to the site's
  values. Re-audit that intersection when the widgets package or the token
  layer changes.

## The Grader

`design-system/lib/grader.ts` owns the Grader contract: the search endpoint
list, the response parser, the suggestion type, and the deep-link builder.
`lib/use-grader-search.ts` is the client hook (debounce, per-session cache,
grouped Google Places / Websites results with caps). `primitives/grader.tsx`
is the only consumer. New Grader behaviour lands in those three files.

## Legal pages and consent

- Terms and Privacy render the backend
  `CompanyInformation.terms_of_service_markdown` and
  `privacy_policy_markdown` fields. `design-system/lib/legal.ts` replaces all
  backend template tokens before rendering.
- Accessibility copy is owned in `design-system/pages/legal-data.ts`.
  `design-system/pages/legal.tsx` is the single composition for all long-form
  legal pages; routes select a document instead of duplicating page markup.
- Render markdown with `react-markdown` and `remark-gfm`. Never inject backend
  HTML.
- The root layout mounts the packaged `CookieConsentModal` once and supplies
  its server-derived `data-consent-regime` through `getConsentRegime()`.
- Use cookie-widget props for behaviour. Because the package exposes no theme
  props, visual overrides are allowed only under `.ks-consent-panel` in
  `design-system/widgets.css`; keep them token-based and clearly commented.

## Dependencies

Motion is CSS, islands are small, and forms are native. The only page-content
rendering dependencies are `react-markdown` and `remark-gfm` for trusted
backend legal markdown. Before adding another dependency, check the existing
islands and the `@keystone-sites/*` packages, prefer the platform, and justify
the addition in the change. Bump the `@keystone-sites/*` packages deliberately
and re-verify the widget surface in the browser after every bump.
